EOF
}

help_tidy_cargo() {
    cat <<'EOF'
Deletes old cargo artifacts under target/ and keeps the current build. Each unit cargo still
builds keeps its newest output: the binary of each package, test harnesses, and each feature
set, such as moqui-core with and without its default features. A later cargo build of unchanged
code does not recompile.

Cargo names a unit's files with a hash of the compiler, the profile, the features, and the
dependency graph. The hash changes when one of those changes, and cargo leaves the previous
files in place. Repeated builds fill target/debug/deps and target/debug/incremental with those
old copies. rustc already drops old incremental sessions inside one of those directories; this
removes the directories for hashes the current build does not use. A build script's run
fingerprint does not record the profile or the features, so each run that a kept library names
stays, along with its output under build/.

It does not remove a package's binary (target/<profile>/moqui) or target/moqui-app. A cargo build
that is running is left alone. load-save does not run this.
EOF
}

help_opensearch_download() {
    cat <<EOF
Downloads opensearch-min for Linux (x64 or arm64) into runtime/opensearch. The version
defaults to $OPENSEARCH_VERSION. An archive already in runtime/ is used instead of downloading.
OpenSearch publishes no macOS tarball; on macOS run it another way and set elasticsearch_url.

Options:
  -f, --force          Replace an existing runtime/opensearch, deleting its data
EOF
}

help_opensearch_start() {
    cat <<'EOF'
Starts runtime/opensearch as a daemon (bin/opensearch -d -p pid). The server uses a node that
answers and only starts its own when nothing does and there is no pid file, so a daemon started
here outlives server restarts.
EOF
}

help_opensearch_stop() {
    echo 'Stops the runtime/opensearch daemon named by its pid file and waits for it to exit.'
}

help_chromium_download() {
    cat <<'EOF'
Downloads a Chromium snapshot build into runtime/chromium, replacing what is there. The server
uses it for PDF when pdf_chromium_path is not set or names no file, before chromium on the PATH;
the browser tests use it too. To use a Chromium installed another way (a distro package, which
is smaller), set pdf_chromium_path to it.

The default build is the one at the main-branch position the current Stable release branched
from (from chromiumdash.appspot.com), or the nearest snapshot before it; it has the Stable code
without the fixes merged to the Stable branch later. A revision argument takes that snapshot.

Snapshots exist for Linux x64 and macOS (x64 and arm64). On Linux it needs the libraries Chromium
uses (nss, nspr, expat, and the like), as the distro chromium package does. Where AppArmor limits
unprivileged user namespaces (Ubuntu 23.10 and later) Chromium cannot start its sandbox without an
AppArmor profile for runtime/chromium/chrome; this task prints how to add one, which needs sudo.

Options:
      --latest         The newest snapshot (tip of tree) instead of the Stable position
EOF
}

help_git() {
    cat <<'EOF'
Runs git with these arguments in the root repo, runtime/ when it is its own repo, and each
repo in runtime/base-component and runtime/component, under a header for each. It keeps going
when one fails, and exits non-zero if any failed. The Gradle git tasks become:
  ./moq git checkout mr-moqui             gitCheckoutAll -Pbranch=mr-moqui
  ./moq git checkout -b new-branch        gitCheckoutAll -Pbranch=new-branch -Pcreate=true
  ./moq git pull upstream master          gitUpstreamAll
